.md
In linear-response theory, the resulting sample signal is the convolution of the <abbr title="impulse response function">IRF</abbr> (or transmission function) and the probe signal (reference):

$$
\\text{sample}(\\tau) = \\text{reference}(\\tau)~\\ast~t(\\tau)
$$

The function $t(\\tau)$, or its Fourier transform $t(\\omega)$, contains all information about the sample.

:::note
We recommend performing all transformations in the time domain whenever possible.
:::

Let's explore this:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
In linear-response theory, the resulting sample signal is the convolution of the <abbr title="impulse response function">IRF</abbr>(or transmission function) and the probe signal (reference):

$$
\\text{sample}(\\tau) = \\text{reference}(\\tau)~\\ast~t(\\tau)
$$

The function $t(\\tau)$, or its Fourier transform $t(\\omega)$, contains all information about the sample.

:::note
We recommend performing all transformations in the time domain whenever possible.
:::

Let's explore this:

