%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Advanced methods
The `"Basic"` method assumes:
$$
\\alpha(\\omega) = - \\frac{16 n_0^2}{(n_0+1)^4} \\frac{2}{d} ~log~|t(\\omega)|,
$$

where $n_0$ is the average refractive index, estimated automatically from the pulse delay.
This equation holds for *optically thick samples* with strong absorption. In the general case, however, $\\alpha$ is:

$$
\\alpha(\\omega) = \\frac{2 \\omega~\\Im~n(\\omega)}{c}
$$

To estimate $n$ from the complex transmission function $t$, one must iteratively solve the Fresnel equations: <cite>[Withayachumnankul, W., Ferguson, B., Rainsford, T., Mickan, S. P., & Abbott, D. (2005). Material parameter extraction for terahertz time-domain spectroscopy using fixed-point iteration. Proceedings of SPIE, 5649, 142–153. doi.org]</cite>

The most general material response for a slab-like sample is modeled as:

$$
t(\\omega)= \\underbrace{\\frac{4 n(\\omega)}{[1+ n(\\omega)]^2}}_{\\text{interface transmission}} \\underbrace{\\exp\\!\\left[i\\frac{\\omega L}{c}\\bigl( n(\\omega)-1\\bigr)\\right]}_{\\text{single pass}} \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{ 1-\\left(\\frac{ n(\\omega)-1}{ n(\\omega)+1}\\right)^2 \\exp\\!\\left(2i\\frac{\\omega L}{c} n(\\omega)\\right) }}_{\\text{Fabry–Pérot term}}
$$


### Phase unwrapping
`TD2FD` stores the principal Fourier phase in the range from $-\\pi$ to $\\pi$. A sampled wrapped phase does not, by itself, tell us how many full $2\\pi$ turns occur between frequency bins. The usual nearest-neighbor unwrap therefore becomes unreliable when the phase changes rapidly or the transmission reaches a deep minimum (for example, near a sharp excitation). This library offers a Kramers–Kronig (KK)-guided method that uses the measured magnitude to help choose those turns.

Here are all the built-in methods for comparison:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Advanced methods
The `"Basic"` method assumes:
$$
\\alpha(\\omega) = - \\frac{16 n_0^2}{(n_0+1)^4} \\frac{2}{d} ~log~|t(\\omega)|,
$$

where $n_0$ is the average refractive index, estimated automatically from the pulse delay.
This equation holds for *optically thick samples* with strong absorption. In the general case, however, $\\alpha$ is:

$$
\\alpha(\\omega) = \\frac{2 \\omega~\\Im~n(\\omega)}{c}
$$

To estimate $n$ from the complex transmission function $t$, one must iteratively solve the Fresnel equations: <cite >[Withayachumnankul, W., Ferguson, B., Rainsford, T., Mickan, S. P., & Abbott, D. (2005). Material parameter extraction for terahertz time-domain spectroscopy using fixed-point iteration. Proceedings of SPIE, 5649, 142–153. doi.org]</cite>

The most general material response for a slab-like sample is modeled as:

$$
t(\\omega)= \\underbrace{\\frac{4 n(\\omega)}{[1+ n(\\omega)]^2}}_{\\text{interface transmission}} \\underbrace{\\exp\\!\\left[i\\frac{\\omega L}{c}\\bigl( n(\\omega)-1\\bigr)\\right]}_{\\text{single pass}} \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{ 1-\\left(\\frac{ n(\\omega)-1}{ n(\\omega)+1}\\right)^2 \\exp\\!\\left(2i\\frac{\\omega L}{c} n(\\omega)\\right) }}_{\\text{Fabry–Pérot term}}
$$


### Phase unwrapping
`TD2FD` stores the principal Fourier phase in the range from $-\\pi$ to $\\pi$. A sampled wrapped phase does not, by itself, tell us how many full $2\\pi$ turns occur between frequency bins. The usual nearest-neighbor unwrap therefore becomes unreliable when the phase changes rapidly or the transmission reaches a deep minimum (for example, near a sharp excitation). This library offers a Kramers–Kronig (KK)-guided method that uses the measured magnitude to help choose those turns.

Here are all the built-in methods for comparison:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
$methods = {None, "Basic", "KK", "KKGuided", "KKGuidedMultiscale"};
UnwrapPhase[t, Method->#, "RemoveLinearPhase"->True] &/@ $methods;
ToStandardOpticsUnits /@ FrequencySpectrumTrim /@ %;
ListLinePlot[%[[All, All, {1,3}]], Frame->True, PlotRange->{{0.25, 3.0}, {-5,20}}, FrameLabel->{"\[Omega] (THz)", "\[Phi]"}, PlotLegends->(ToString/@$methods), PlotLabel->"Phase unwrapping"]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Legended[ToExpression[FrontEndRef["F81198e4a-2bba-4332-adbc-d0d1b92feaf8"], InputForm], Placed[LineLegend[{Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.24, 0.6, 0.8], AbsoluteThickness[2]], Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.95, 0.627, 0.1425], AbsoluteThickness[2]], Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.455, 0.7, 0.21], AbsoluteThickness[2]], Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.922526, 0.385626, 0.209179], AbsoluteThickness[2]], Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.578, 0.51, 0.85], AbsoluteThickness[2]]}, {"None", "Basic", "KK", "KKGuided", "KKGuidedMultiscale"}, LegendMarkers -> {{False, Automatic}, {False, Automatic}, {False, Automatic}, {False, Automatic}, {False, Automatic}}, Joined -> {True, True, True, True, True}, LabelStyle -> {}, LegendLayout -> "Column"], After, Identity]])(*,*)(*"1:"*)(*]VB*)

