.md
### Fabry–Pérot deconvolution
Finally, to take full advantage of the time-domain method, we should deconvolve the material parameters from the internal reflections that produce oscillatory patterns in all spectra. To enable deconvolution, simply set:


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Fabry–Pérot deconvolution
Finally, to take full advantage of the time-domain method, we should deconvolve the material parameters from the internal reflections that produce oscillatory patterns in all spectra. To enable deconvolution, simply set:


%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Method->{"Iterative", "SolveFP"->True}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
However, this usually does not work on the first attempt. ==FP deconvolution is extremely sensitive to slight inaccuracies in thickness measurements or differences in aperture between the sample and reference==. Therefore, we need to vary `"Gain"` and thickness slightly, as well as the static phase shift `"PhaseCorrection"`. Note that `"PhaseCorrection"` can only be $\\pm Z ~ 2\\pi$; it originates from noisy low-frequency data, which can produce such phase jumps. This is essentially a multivariable optimization problem.

To find the best (or true) combination, we use an estimated value generated by `EstimateMaterialParameter["FP Reduction"]`, which is essentially the relative amplitude of the FP oscillations compared with the background. Here, we find it using a grid search:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
However, this usually does not work on the first attempt. ==FP deconvolution is extremely sensitive to slight inaccuracies in thickness measurements or differences in aperture between the sample and reference==. Therefore, we need to vary `"Gain"` and thickness slightly, as well as the static phase shift `"PhaseCorrection"`. Note that `"PhaseCorrection"` can only be $\\pm Z ~ 2\\pi$; it originates from noisy low-frequency data, which can produce such phase jumps. This is essentially a multivariable optimization problem.

To find the best (or true) combination, we use an estimated value generated by `EstimateMaterialParameter["FP Reduction"]`, which is essentially the relative amplitude of the FP oscillations compared with the background. Here, we find it using a grid search:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Module[{band = Interval[{(*VB[*)(Quantity[1.5, "Terahertz"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KWnMIB4vkAjLTC13SU3OL0osyS8K5gCKBJYm5pVkllQWMYDBD/tgTiAVklqUmJFaVFIFAKruE0k="*)(*]VB*), (*VB[*)(Quantity[2.5, "Terahertz"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KWnMIB4vkAjLTC13SU3OL0osyS8K5gCKBJYm5pVkllQWMYABi0MwJ5AKSS1KzEgtKqkCAJu9ElY="*)(*]VB*)}], temporal = {}},
  Monitor[Table[
    temporal = Append[temporal, {d, gain, EstimateMaterialParameter["FP Reduction", unwrapped,
      Quantity[d, "Millimeters"], "Gain" -> gain, "FPInterval" -> band]}];
    temporal[[-1]],
    {d, 0.6, 0.7, 0.0025}, {gain, 0.5,1.6,0.05}], ListDensityPlot[temporal, 
      PlotRange->{{0.6,0.7}, {0.5,1.6}}]
  ]
];
ListContourPlot[data = Flatten[%, 1], 
  FrameLabel->{"Thickness (mm)", "Gain"}, PlotRange->Full]
  
MaximalBy[data, Last]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["F20cc474e-058d-4d48-a981-1a92b6bd38b1"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgUJGxkkJ5uYm6TqGphapOiapJhY6CZaWhjqGiZaGiWZJaUYWyQZAgCVIBW3"*)(*]VB*)

